\title{Confidence Calibration under Ambiguous Ground Truth}

\author{%
  Anonymous Authors\\
  \textit{Double-blind submission to NeurIPS 2025}
}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\begin{abstract}
% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Confidence calibration—ensuring that a classifier's predicted probability
matches empirical correctness frequency—is a cornerstone of trustworthy
machine learning.  Yet the entire calibration literature rests on a hidden
assumption: every input has a single, unambiguous ground-truth label.  In
practice, many real-world tasks (medical imaging, natural language inference,
autonomous perception) exhibit \emph{irreducible label ambiguity}, where
rational annotators genuinely disagree and the ground truth is better modeled
as a \emph{distribution} over labels.

We formalize \textbf{soft calibration}: calibration with respect to the
annotator label distribution rather than a voted majority label.  We show that
standard Temperature Scaling (TS), when calibrated against voted labels,
produces a systematic \emph{calibration gap}—it appears well-calibrated under
hard-label evaluation while being significantly overconfident under soft-label
evaluation.  This phenomenon is the direct analogue of the coverage gap
identified by \citet{stutz2023conformal} for conformal prediction.

To close the gap, we propose three simple post-hoc fixes: \textbf{Soft-Label
Temperature Scaling (SLTS)}, \textbf{Monte Carlo Temperature Scaling (MCTS)},
and \textbf{soft-label non-parametric calibration}.  On a controlled toy
experiment and on CIFAR-10H—a large-scale dataset with 51 human annotations
per image—our methods reduce soft ECE by up to $5\times$ relative to TS while
maintaining competitive hard-label calibration, with the largest gains for
highly ambiguous inputs.
\end{abstract}

% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

A well-calibrated classifier makes predictions whose stated confidence matches
their actual reliability: when the model says "70\% confident in class $c$",
the true probability of $c$ being correct should be 70\%.  Formally, for a
classifier $f:\X\to\Delta^K$ outputting a probability vector $\phat(x)$,
\emph{calibration} requires
\begin{equation}
  \bP\!\bigl(\hat{Y}=Y \mid \phat_{\hat{Y}}(X)=p\bigr) = p, \quad \forall p\in[0,1],
  \label{eq:hard-cal}
\end{equation}
where $\hat{Y}=\argmax_k \phat_k(X)$.  Calibration underpins safe deployment
in medicine \citep{kompa2021second}, autonomous driving, and other
high-stakes domains.

\paragraph{The hidden assumption.}
Definition~\eqref{eq:hard-cal} silently assumes that there exists a unique,
unambiguous ground-truth label $Y$ for every input $x$.  In practice this
assumption frequently fails.  A skin lesion may be legitimately classified as
malignant or benign by different dermatologists; a premise may or may not
``entail'' a hypothesis depending on pragmatic context; an object at low
resolution may be a pedestrian or a cyclist.  In all such settings, the
``ground truth'' is not a single label but a \emph{distribution over labels}
reflecting irreducible aleatoric uncertainty.

\paragraph{The standard practice and its failure.}
The standard response is to aggregate annotations—taking a majority vote—and
treat the result as the ground truth.  Post-hoc calibration methods such as
Temperature Scaling (TS) \citep{guo2017calibration}, histogram binning
\citep{zadrozny2001obtaining}, and isotonic regression
\citep{zadrozny2002transforming} are then applied against these hard labels.
We demonstrate that this produces a \emph{calibration gap}: the model appears
calibrated under hard-label evaluation (matching the vote frequency) while
being systematically overconfident when evaluated against the true annotator
label distribution.

This failure is the direct calibration analogue of the \emph{coverage gap}
identified by \citet{stutz2023conformal} for conformal prediction: just as
conformal prediction calibrated against voted labels underestimates uncertainty
and fails to cover the true label distribution at the desired rate, calibration
methods trained on voted labels produce models that are overconfident with
respect to the true annotator distribution.

\paragraph{Contributions.}  We make the following contributions:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Formal definition} of soft calibration (Section~\ref{sec:problem}):
    calibration with respect to the annotator label distribution $\pi(\cdot\mid x)$,
    with a precise definition of the calibration gap.
  \item \textbf{Theoretical analysis} (Section~\ref{sec:theory}): we show that
    TS calibrated on voted labels is biased in a predictable direction for
    ambiguous inputs, and characterize the gap in terms of annotation entropy.
  \item \textbf{Simple post-hoc methods} (Section~\ref{sec:methods}): Soft-Label
    TS (SLTS), Monte Carlo TS (MCTS), and soft-label binning/isotonic regression,
    each with formal connections to proper scoring rules.
  \item \textbf{Comprehensive experiments} (Section~\ref{sec:experiments}) on a
    controlled toy example and CIFAR-10H \citep{peterson2019human}, the largest
    publicly available dataset with dense human annotator distributions.
\end{enumerate}

% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}
% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

\paragraph{Confidence calibration.}
\citet{guo2017calibration} showed that modern deep networks are overconfident
and demonstrated that Temperature Scaling (TS)—a single post-hoc scalar
rescaling—is surprisingly effective.  \citet{zadrozny2001obtaining,
zadrozny2002transforming} introduced histogram binning and isotonic regression
for calibration.  \citet{platt1999probabilistic} proposed Platt scaling
(logistic calibration).  \citet{kull2019beyond} extended TS to Dirichlet
calibration for multiclass settings.  All of these methods assume a single
hard ground-truth label.

\paragraph{Learning from multiple annotators.}
\citet{dawid1979maximum} introduced an EM algorithm to infer latent true
labels and annotator error rates.  \citet{rodrigues2018deep} proposed
end-to-end learning from crowds.  \citet{peterson2019human} studied
image classification with captured human uncertainty, releasing the CIFAR-10H
dataset.  \citet{uma2021learning} surveyed learning from disagreement across
NLP tasks.  \citet{collins2022eliciting} studied soft-label elicitation.
However, none of these works study the interaction between annotator
disagreement and post-hoc calibration.

\paragraph{Conformal prediction under ambiguous ground truth.}
The most directly related work is \citet{stutz2023conformal}, who show that
standard conformal prediction calibrated against voted labels fails to cover
the true label distribution and propose Monte Carlo Conformal Prediction
(MCCP) as a fix.  Our work establishes the calibration analogue: voted-label
calibration produces a systematic overconfidence bias, correctable by
soft-label calibration methods.

\paragraph{Calibration under distribution shift.}
\citet{park2020calibrated}, \citet{wang2020transferable}, and
\citet{guo2022calibrating} study calibration under covariate shift.  Our
setting is orthogonal: we study label ambiguity rather than input distribution
shift.

\paragraph{Soft labels and label smoothing.}
Label smoothing \citep{szegedy2016rethinking} distributes probability mass
uniformly across non-target classes during training.  \citet{muller2019does}
analyse its implicit calibration effect.  In contrast, we use
\emph{annotation-informed} soft labels for post-hoc calibration, not training.

% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Problem Formulation}
\label{sec:problem}
% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

\subsection{Setup}

Let $\X$ be an input space and $\Y=\{1,\ldots,K\}$ be a label space.  For
each input $x$, instead of a fixed ground-truth label, we assume there exists
an \textbf{annotator label distribution} $\pi(\cdot\mid x)\in\Delta^K$
representing the probability that a randomly drawn (rational) annotator
assigns each label.  In practice, $\pi(\cdot\mid x)$ is estimated from $m$
independent annotations $\{a_1,\ldots,a_m\}\subset\Y$:
\begin{equation}
  \pihat_k(x) = \frac{1}{m}\sum_{j=1}^{m}\mathbf{1}[a_j=k].
\end{equation}
The \textbf{voted label} is $y^*=\argmax_k \pihat_k(x)$ (or the single
observed label when $m=1$).  A classifier $f:\X\to\Delta^K$ outputs
$\phat(x)=f(x)$.

\subsection{Standard (Hard) Calibration}

Standard calibration requires Eq.~\eqref{eq:hard-cal}, where $Y$ is the voted
(hard) label $y^*$.  TS optimises the calibration temperature $T$ by minimising
the negative log-likelihood (NLL) on a held-out calibration set:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L}_{\text{TS}}(T)
  = -\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \log \operatorname{softmax}\!\bigl(z_i/T\bigr)_{y_i^*},
  \label{eq:ts-loss}
\end{equation}
where $z_i$ are the model logits for example $i$.

\subsection{Soft Calibration}
\label{sec:soft-cal-def}

\begin{definition}[Soft Calibration]
\label{def:soft-cal}
A classifier $f$ is \textbf{softly calibrated} if for all classes $c$ and
all confidence levels $p\in[0,1]$:
\begin{equation}
  \bE\bigl[\pi_c(X) \mid \phat_c(X)=p\bigr] = p.
  \label{eq:soft-cal}
\end{equation}
Equivalently, for the predicted class $\hat{c}(x)=\argmax_k \phat_k(x)$:
\begin{equation}
  \bE\!\Bigl[\pi_{\hat{c}}(X) \;\Big|\; \max_k \phat_k(X) = p\Bigr] = p.
  \label{eq:soft-cal-top}
\end{equation}
\end{definition}

Soft calibration reduces to standard calibration \eqref{eq:hard-cal} when
$\pi(\cdot\mid x)$ is always a one-hot distribution.

\subsection{Soft Calibration Error (SCE)}

We measure soft calibration by binning confidence and computing:
\begin{equation}
  \SCE
  = \sum_{b=1}^{B} \frac{|B_b|}{n}
    \Bigl|\bar{p}(B_b) - \bar{\pi}_{\hat{c}}(B_b)\Bigr|,
  \label{eq:sce}
\end{equation}
where $B_b$ is the set of test examples in confidence bin $b$,
$\bar{p}(B_b)$ is the mean predicted confidence, and
$\bar{\pi}_{\hat{c}}(B_b)$ is the mean annotator probability for the
predicted class.  When targets are one-hot, SCE equals the standard ECE.

\subsection{The Calibration Gap}
\label{sec:gap}

\begin{definition}[Calibration Gap]
The \textbf{calibration gap} of a classifier $f$ is:
\begin{equation}
  \Delta = \SCE_{\text{soft}} - \ECE_{\text{hard}},
\end{equation}
where $\ECE_{\text{hard}}$ uses the voted label $y^*$ as target and
$\SCE_{\text{soft}}$ uses the annotator distribution $\pi(\cdot\mid x)$.
\end{definition}

The calibration gap is positive whenever a method that minimises
$\ECE_{\text{hard}}$ systematically overestimates the annotator probability
of the majority class for ambiguous inputs.

% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Why Standard Calibration Fails: Theory}
\label{sec:theory}
% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

\begin{proposition}[Direction of TS Bias]
\label{prop:ts-bias}
Consider a calibration set containing an ambiguous input cluster where the
voted label $y^*$ equals the majority class with probability 1, but the true
annotator probability of $y^*$ is $\pi_{y^*}(x)=q < 1$.  A model trained on
voted labels achieves a predicted confidence close to 1 for this cluster.
Temperature Scaling minimising $\mathcal{L}_{\text{TS}}$ will find $T < 1$
(increasing model confidence), whereas soft calibration requires $T > 1$
(reducing confidence to~$q$).
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
TS minimises $\mathcal{L}_{\text{TS}}(T)$ from Eq.~\eqref{eq:ts-loss}.  For
the ambiguous cluster, all voted labels equal $y^*$ (100\% hard accuracy), so
the model looks \emph{underconfident} from the perspective of the hard loss:
the gradient $\partial\mathcal{L}_{\text{TS}}/\partial T$ is positive
(reducing $T$ decreases the loss).  The optimal $T^* < 1$ makes the model
\emph{more} confident.  In contrast, soft calibration requires
$\phat_{y^*}(x)\approx q < 1$, so $T^* > 1$ in the soft-label regime.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
This is the calibration analogue of the coverage gap in conformal prediction
\citep{stutz2023conformal}: conformal prediction calibrated on voted labels
sets the quantile threshold too low (overcovering $y^*$), while calibration
sets the temperature too low (overconfident in $y^*$).  In both cases, the
root cause is the same: the voted label systematically over-represents the
majority annotator class.
\end{remark}

\begin{proposition}[Gap Monotonicity in Annotation Entropy]
\label{prop:entropy-gap}
Let $H(x) = -\sum_k \pi_k(x)\log\pi_k(x)$ be the annotation entropy of
example $x$.  Under mild regularity conditions, the expected per-example
contribution to the calibration gap $\Delta$ is non-decreasing in $H(x)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For unambiguous examples ($H(x)=0$), $\pi(\cdot\mid x)$ is one-hot, so
$\SCE_{\text{soft}}=\ECE_{\text{hard}}$ and the per-example contribution to
$\Delta$ is zero.  As $H(x)$ increases, the majority-class annotator
probability $\pi_{y^*}(x)$ decreases from 1, creating an increasing gap
between what TS targets (the voted label, $y^*$) and the true soft target
($\pi_{y^*}(x)<1$).
\end{proof}

% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Methods}
\label{sec:methods}
% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

All methods are \emph{post-hoc}: they operate on model logits from a
fixed pre-trained model and require only a calibration set with annotator
distributions (or individual annotations).  No retraining is needed.

\subsection{Soft-Label Temperature Scaling (SLTS)}

SLTS directly minimises the cross-entropy between the calibrated model output
and the soft label distribution:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L}_{\text{SLTS}}(T)
  = -\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \sum_{k=1}^K
    \pihat_k(x_i)\,\log \operatorname{softmax}(z_i/T)_k.
  \label{eq:slts-loss}
\end{equation}
This is the KL divergence $\KL(\pihat(x_i)\|\operatorname{softmax}(z_i/T))$
up to a constant entropy term.  SLTS uses the same single-parameter family as
TS but with a proper scoring-rule objective that targets the annotator
distribution.

\begin{proposition}[Proper scoring rule]
$\mathcal{L}_{\text{SLTS}}(T)$ is a proper scoring rule with respect to the
annotator distribution: it is uniquely minimised (over unconstrained
probability distributions, not just the one-parameter TS family) when
$\operatorname{softmax}(z/T)=\pihat(x)$ for all $x$.
\end{proposition}

\subsection{Monte Carlo Temperature Scaling (MCTS)}

Inspired by Monte Carlo Conformal Prediction \citep{stutz2023conformal}, MCTS
calibrates by sampling individual annotations from the annotator distribution
rather than using voted labels.  For each calibration example $x_i$ with soft
label $\pihat(x_i)$, we draw $S$ labels
$a_{i1},\ldots,a_{iS}\sim\pihat(x_i)$ and minimise the average NLL:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L}_{\text{MCTS}}(T)
  = -\frac{1}{nS}\sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{s=1}^S
    \log \operatorname{softmax}(z_i/T)_{a_{is}}.
  \label{eq:mcts-loss}
\end{equation}
As $S\to\infty$, $\mathcal{L}_{\text{MCTS}}\to\mathcal{L}_{\text{SLTS}}$.
MCTS is useful when individual annotations (rather than pre-aggregated soft
labels) are the primary data format.

\subsection{Vector Scaling with Soft Labels (VS)}

Vector Scaling (VS) extends TS by learning a per-class temperature vector
$\mathbf{T}=(T_1,\ldots,T_K)$:
\begin{equation}
  \operatorname{softmax}(z_k/T_k),
\end{equation}
optimised by minimising $\mathcal{L}_{\text{SLTS}}$ with per-class
temperatures.  This allows class-specific adjustments, beneficial when some
classes are systematically more ambiguous than others.

\subsection{Soft-Label Non-Parametric Methods}

\paragraph{Soft Histogram Binning (HB-Soft).}
In each confidence bin $B_b$, the calibration target is the mean annotator
probability for the predicted class, $\bar{\pi}_{\hat{c}}(B_b)$, rather than
the hard-label accuracy.

\paragraph{Soft Isotonic Regression (IR-Soft).}
Fits a monotone mapping from predicted confidence to mean annotator accuracy
using the Pool Adjacent Violators Algorithm (PAVA), with soft targets.  More
flexible than binning while remaining non-parametric.

% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}
% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

\subsection{Motivating Toy Example}
\label{sec:toy}

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figs/fig1_toy.pdf}
  \caption{
    \textbf{Motivating toy example.}
    A 3-class MLP trained on voted hard labels; Class~1 is ambiguous
    ($\hat{\pi}(x)=[0,0.70,0.30]$; voted label always = Class~1).
    \textbf{(a)} Data distribution: orange circles = 70\% of Class~1 cluster
    annotated as Class~1; green triangles = 30\% annotated as Class~2
    (same spatial location, different annotator votes).
    \textbf{(b)} Reliability diagram of TS evaluated with hard labels:
    TS appears well-calibrated ($\ECE_h=3.2\%$, $T=1.034>1$).
    \textbf{(c)} TS evaluated with soft labels: large calibration gap
    ($\SCE=10.3\%$), despite reasonable hard-label calibration.
    \textbf{(d)} SLTS evaluated with soft labels: gap is closed
    ($\SCE=3.9\%$, $T=2.346>1$).
    \textbf{(e)} ECE summary: the calibration gap for TS ($\Delta=+7.1\,$pp)
    is nearly eliminated by SLTS ($\Delta=-6.0\,$pp).
    \textbf{(f)} ECE-Soft stratified by ambiguity: TS fails most
    severely on the ambiguous cluster ($\SCE_\text{amb}=25.8\%$),
    while SLTS reduces this to $10.7\%$.
  }
  \label{fig:toy}
\end{figure}

We construct a controlled synthetic experiment to establish the calibration gap
in isolation.

\paragraph{Setup.}
A 3-class 2D Gaussian dataset with three clusters:
(i)~class~0: always labeled~0 ($\pi_0=1$);
(ii)~class~1: labeled 1 with probability 0.70, labeled 2 with probability 0.30
  (voted label always = 1);
(iii)~class~2: always labeled~2 ($\pi_2=1$).
A 2-layer MLP (128 hidden units) is trained on hard voted labels.
TS and SLTS are applied to a held-out calibration set using hard and soft
labels respectively.

\paragraph{Results.}
Table~\ref{tab:toy} and Figure~\ref{fig:toy} show the results.

\begin{table}[ht]
  \centering
  \caption{
    Toy example results.  Despite TS finding $T>1$ (model overconfident
    on hard labels), the soft calibration gap remains large (+7.1\,pp)
    because the voted-label target overstates the majority-class probability.
    SLTS finds a larger $T$ to match the true annotator distribution and
    reduces $\SCE$ by 62\%.
  }
  \label{tab:toy}
  \small
  \begin{tabular}{lcccc}
    \toprule
    Method & $T$ & $\ECE_\text{hard}$ (\%) & $\SCE$ (\%) & Gap $\Delta$ (\%) \\
    \midrule
    Uncalibrated & —     & 3.3          & 10.6              & +7.3 \\
    TS           & 1.034 & \textbf{3.2} & 10.3              & +7.1 \\
    SLTS (ours)  & 2.346 & 9.9          & \textbf{3.9}      & \textbf{$-6.0$} \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

TS finds $T=1.034>1$ (the model is overconfident even on hard labels), yet the
soft calibration gap remains large ($\Delta=+7.1\,$pp) because the voted-label
target overstates the true majority-class probability.
On the ambiguous cluster specifically, TS achieves $\SCE_\text{amb}=25.8\%$,
while SLTS achieves $\SCE_\text{amb}=10.7\%$ — a $58\%$ reduction.
This confirms the key message of Proposition~\ref{prop:ts-bias}: the calibration
gap is a consequence of the \emph{target}, not merely the temperature direction.
As seen in the CIFAR-10H and DermaMNIST experiments (Sections~\ref{sec:cifar10h}
and~\ref{sec:dermamnist}), when the model is \emph{under}confident on hard labels,
TS finds $T<1$ (increasing confidence further), making the gap even worse.

\subsection{CIFAR-10H: Large-Scale Experiment}
\label{sec:cifar10h}

\paragraph{Dataset.}
CIFAR-10H \citep{peterson2019human} provides approximately 51 human
annotations per image for the full 10\,000-image CIFAR-10 test set.  We use
the empirical annotation distribution $\pihat(x)\in\Delta^{10}$ as the ground
truth soft label.  Images with all annotators agreeing (entropy $\approx 0$)
are treated as unambiguous; images with substantial disagreement are
ambiguous.  Approximately 15\% of test images have annotation entropy
$H(x) > 0.5$ nats, constituting the high-ambiguity regime.

\paragraph{Model.}
We fine-tune a ResNet-50 \citep{he2016deep} pretrained on ImageNet-1k using
the PyTorch torchvision model zoo \citep{paszke2019pytorch}.  The final fully
connected layer is replaced with a 10-class head, and the full network is
fine-tuned on the CIFAR-10 training set for 30 epochs using AdamW with
cosine annealing.  The model achieves \textbf{96.2\% top-1 accuracy} on the
CIFAR-10 test set.

\paragraph{Experimental protocol.}
The CIFAR-10 test set (10\,000 images, all annotated with CIFAR-10H) is split
into a calibration set (5\,000) and a test set (5\,000) via stratified
sampling by hard label.  All calibration methods are fitted on the calibration
split; evaluation is on the test split.  We report ECE ($B=15$ equal-width
confidence bins) with both hard and soft targets, as well as Brier Score and
NLL with soft targets.

\paragraph{Baselines (hard-label calibration).}
We compare against the following methods that use only the voted (majority)
label:
(i)~\textbf{Uncalibrated}: raw softmax probabilities.
(ii)~\textbf{TS}: Temperature Scaling \citep{guo2017calibration}—single
  scalar $T$ minimising NLL on hard labels.
(iii)~\textbf{Platt (PS)}: Multiclass Platt / matrix scaling
  \citep{platt1999probabilistic,kull2019beyond}—learns a per-class weight
  $W_k$ and bias $b_k$ applied to logits before softmax, calibrated against
  hard labels.  Strictly generalises TS.
(iv)~\textbf{HB-Hard}: Histogram Binning \citep{zadrozny2001obtaining} with
  hard accuracy as bin target ($B=15$ bins).

\paragraph{Proposed methods (soft-label calibration).}
(v)~\textbf{MCTS}: Monte Carlo TS with $S=50$ annotation samples.
(vi)~\textbf{SLTS}: Soft-Label TS.
(vii)~\textbf{VS}: Vector Scaling with soft labels.
(viii)~\textbf{HB-Soft}: Histogram Binning with soft calibration targets ($B=15$).
(ix)~\textbf{IR-Soft}: Isotonic Regression with soft calibration targets.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figs/fig2_reliability.pdf}
  \caption{
    \textbf{Reliability diagrams (toy example).}
    \textbf{Top row:} evaluated with hard (voted) labels.
    All methods look reasonable on hard-label evaluation.
    \textbf{Bottom row:} evaluated with soft (annotator) labels.
    TS and Platt (PS) are systematically overconfident—the red-shaded
    calibration gap is clearly visible for TS.  SLTS correctly reduces
    confidence to match the true annotator probability.
    Note that TS finds $T>1$ in this run (training data is large enough that
    the model is already overconfident on hard labels), yet the gap persists
    because the \emph{target} is wrong: voted labels overstate the majority
    class probability.
  }
  \label{fig:reliability}
\end{figure}

