    \multicolumn{7}{l}{\textit{Soft-label methods (ours)}} \\
    MCTS          & 2.81 & 2.74 & 3.58 & 0.058 & 0.195 & +0.84 \\
    SLTS          & 2.94 & 3.12 & \textbf{3.23} & \textbf{0.054} & \textbf{0.187} & \textbf{+0.11} \\
    VS            & —    & 3.45 & 3.87 & 0.061 & 0.203 & +0.42 \\
    HB-Soft       & —    & 3.67 & 3.91 & 0.063 & — & +0.24 \\
    IR-Soft       & —    & 3.21 & 3.35 & 0.055 & 0.188 & +0.14 \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Main results.}
Table~\ref{tab:main} presents the core findings.

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{All hard-label baselines fail.}  TS, Platt scaling, and HB-Hard
  achieve excellent $\ECE_h$ (1.83--2.31\%) but leave $\SCE$ virtually
  unchanged (8.51--8.88\%), \emph{widening} the calibration gap to
  $\Delta=+6.46$--$+6.93$ pp.  Crucially, Platt scaling—despite its greater
  flexibility (per-class $W_k$, $b_k$)—cannot close the gap because its
  objective is still calibrated on voted labels.  This confirms that the
  calibration gap is a fundamental consequence of the loss target, not
  of model capacity.

\item \textbf{Soft calibration closes the gap.}  All our proposed methods
  reduce $\SCE$ dramatically: SLTS achieves $\SCE=3.23\%$ (63\% relative
  reduction vs.\ TS), and IR-Soft achieves $\SCE=3.35\%$, while maintaining
  competitive $\ECE_h$ (within 2 pp of TS).

\item \textbf{Proper-scoring-rule metrics.}  The advantage of our methods is
  confirmed by Brier score (0.054 vs.\ 0.092 for SLTS vs.\ TS) and NLL
  (0.187 vs.\ 0.304), both strongly favouring soft calibration.

\item \textbf{Temperature direction.}  TS and Platt find effective temperatures
  below 1 (increasing confidence); SLTS finds $T=2.94>1$ (decreasing
  confidence).  The direction reversal directly confirms
  Proposition~\ref{prop:ts-bias}.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{figs/fig3_summary.pdf}
  \caption{
    \textbf{ECE-Hard vs.\ ECE-Soft on CIFAR-10H.}
    Hatched bars: hard-label ECE; solid bars: soft-label ECE.  TS (orange)
    appears well-calibrated on hard labels but exhibits a large calibration gap
    on soft labels (annotated with a double-headed arrow).  All proposed
    methods (green, purple, yellow) substantially reduce soft ECE while
    maintaining competitive hard ECE.
  }
  \label{fig:summary}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Stratified Analysis by Ambiguity Level}

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.75\linewidth]{figs/fig4_stratified.pdf}
  \caption{
    \textbf{ECE-Soft stratified by annotation entropy quartile.}
    Q1 = lowest ambiguity (entropy $\approx 0$); Q4 = highest ambiguity.
    The calibration gap between TS (orange) and our methods (SLTS: green;
    MCTS: purple) grows with ambiguity, consistent with
    Proposition~\ref{prop:entropy-gap}.
  }
  \label{fig:stratified}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:stratified} shows $\SCE$ stratified by annotation entropy
quartile.  Key findings:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Low ambiguity (Q1):} All methods perform similarly
    ($\SCE\approx 1$–$2\%$), since unambiguous examples have one-hot
    distributions and soft calibration reduces to hard calibration.
  \item \textbf{High ambiguity (Q4):} TS degrades significantly
    ($\SCE\approx 18\%$) while SLTS maintains $\SCE\approx 6\%$.  This
    3$\times$ gap confirms that the calibration failure is concentrated in
    high-ambiguity inputs.
  \item The monotone increase of TS's $\SCE$ with annotation entropy directly
    confirms Proposition~\ref{prop:entropy-gap}.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Effect of Number of Annotations}
\label{sec:n-annotators}

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.80\linewidth]{figs/fig5_n_annotators.pdf}
  \caption{
    \textbf{ECE-Soft vs.\ number of annotations $m$ per example (toy example).}
    Shaded band: $\pm 1$ standard deviation over 7 random seeds.
    SLTS improves monotonically with $m$, while TS (dashed) is insensitive
    since it discards annotation uncertainty and uses only the voted label.
    Already $m=3$ annotations substantially reduce ECE-Soft relative to TS.
  }
  \label{fig:n_annotators}
\end{figure}

CIFAR-10H provides up to 51 annotations per image.  We study how many
annotations are needed for effective soft calibration by subsampling $m$
annotations per image ($m \in \{1, 3, 5, 10, 20, 51\}$) to estimate
$\pihat(x)$, then fitting SLTS with the resulting soft labels.  With a
single annotation ($m=1$), SLTS degenerates to TS (all soft labels are
one-hot), and $\SCE\approx 8.9\%$.  With $m=5$ annotations, $\SCE$ drops to
4.5\%; with $m=10$, to 3.7\%; and with all 51 annotations, to 3.23\%.  This
suggests that even modest multi-annotation effort ($m\ge 5$) substantially
improves soft calibration.

\subsection{Monte Carlo Temperature Scaling: Convergence Analysis}
\label{sec:mcts-analysis}

MCTS approximates SLTS by sampling $S$ annotations per example from the
estimated annotator distribution $\pihat(x)$.  Theoretically, since
$\mathbb{E}_{\hat{y}\sim\pihat}\bigl[\mathrm{CE}(p(\cdot\mid z,T),\,\hat{y})\bigr]
= \mathrm{KL}(\pihat \,\|\, p(\cdot\mid z,T)) + H(\pihat)$,
the MCTS objective converges to the SLTS objective (in expectation) as
$S\to\infty$, with variance $\propto 1/S$ by the LLN.

Table~\ref{tab:mcts} reports this convergence empirically on the toy example
(10 random seeds per $S$ value).  Key findings:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Rapid mean convergence.} Even $S=1$ matches the ECE-Soft of
    SLTS ($S\to\infty$): $5.09\%$ vs.\ $5.10\%$.  This is not surprising
    given $N=2{,}000$ calibration examples — the large-$N$ regime makes
    per-sample Monte Carlo noise average out.
  \item \textbf{Variance $\propto 1/\sqrt{S}$.} The standard deviation of
    $T^*$ over seeds drops from $\pm 0.151$ at $S=1$ to $\pm 0.007$ at
    $S=200$, consistent with the MC rate.
  \item \textbf{Practical recommendation.} $S=20$--$50$ is sufficient: beyond
    $S=20$ the ECE-Soft variance is negligible ($\sigma < 0.3\%$) while the
    computational cost remains linear in $S$.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{table}[ht]
  \centering
  \caption{
    \textbf{MCTS convergence on the toy example.}
    Mean $\pm$ std over 10 random seeds; $S$ = number of MC annotation samples
    per calibration example.  $S=1$ already matches SLTS in expectation;
    variance vanishes rapidly.
  }
  \label{tab:mcts}
  \small
  \setlength{\tabcolsep}{6pt}
  \begin{tabular}{lcc}
    \toprule
    Method & $\SCE$ (\%) & $T^*$ \\
    \midrule
    Uncalibrated          & 8.48          & —                    \\
    TS (hard labels)      & 7.44          & $1.097$              \\
    \midrule
    MCTS $S=1$            & $5.09\pm1.03$ & $2.581\pm0.151$      \\
    MCTS $S=5$            & $5.09\pm0.34$ & $2.594\pm0.052$      \\
    MCTS $S=20$           & $5.02\pm0.30$ & $2.586\pm0.044$      \\
    MCTS $S=50$           & $5.06\pm0.14$ & $2.586\pm0.027$      \\
    MCTS $S=200$          & $5.05\pm0.04$ & $2.585\pm0.007$      \\
    SLTS ($S\to\infty$)   & 5.10          & $2.587$              \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Ablation: Calibration Set Size}

We vary the calibration set size from 250 to 5\,000 examples (sampling
stratified by class).  TS's soft ECE remains around 8.5--8.8\% regardless of
calibration set size, confirming that the calibration gap is a fundamental
bias rather than a variance issue.  SLTS improves with more calibration data,
from $\SCE=4.1\%$ at $n=250$ to $\SCE=3.23\%$ at $n=5\,000$.

% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\subsection{Skin Disease Classification (DermaMNIST)}
\label{sec:dermamnist}
% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Analogous to the skin-condition case study in \citet{stutz2023conformal}, we
evaluate our methods on a medical imaging dataset with realistic annotator
disagreement.  We use \textbf{DermaMNIST} \citep{yang2023medmnist}, a 7-class
skin lesion classification benchmark derived from HAM10000
\citep{tschandl2018ham10000}, with classes: actinic keratoses (AK), basal cell
carcinoma (BCC), benign keratosis-like lesions (BKL), dermatofibroma (DF),
melanoma (Mel), melanocytic nevi (NV), and vascular lesions (Vasc).

\paragraph{Annotator model.}
Since DermaMNIST provides only consensus (voted) labels, we simulate
multi-annotator disagreement using a clinically calibrated $7\times 7$ confusion
matrix (overall inter-reader agreement $\approx 64.7\%$), consistent with
published dermatologist accuracy on this task \citep{haenssle2018man}.
The most confusable class pair is \emph{melanoma}~vs.~\emph{melanocytic nevi}
(Mel$\leftrightarrow$NV, agreement $\approx 63\%$/$70\%$ respectively), which
is also clinically the most consequential distinction.  Each test image receives
$K=5$ synthetic annotator labels, yielding soft distributions $\pihat(x)$.

\paragraph{Setup.}
We fine-tune a ResNet-18 \citep{he2016deep} pretrained on ImageNet-1k with
class-weighted cross-entropy to handle class imbalance (NV constitutes
$\approx 67\%$ of training images).  The validation split (1\,003 images) serves
as the calibration set; the test split (2\,005 images) for evaluation.

