\paragraph{Results.}
Table~\ref{tab:derm} and Figure~\ref{fig:derm} show results that closely
parallel CIFAR-10H, validating that the calibration gap is a general
phenomenon:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Direction of TS temperature.}  TS finds $T=0.88<1$ (increasing
  confidence), while SLTS finds $T=2.75>1$ (decreasing confidence).  This
  direction reversal directly confirms Proposition~\ref{prop:ts-bias} in the
  medical imaging setting.

\item \textbf{Hard-label baselines fail.}  TS and Platt reduce $\ECE_h$ to
  $2.1$--$2.4\%$ but leave $\SCE$ at $10.5$--$10.8\%$, widening the
  calibration gap to $\Delta\approx 8$--$9$ pp.

\item \textbf{Soft-label methods close the gap.}  SLTS reduces $\SCE$ to
  $4.6\%$ ($57\%$ relative reduction vs.\ TS) and IR-Soft to $4.8\%$.
  The Brier score and NLL improvements are similarly large (Brier:
  $0.076$ vs.\ $0.136$ for SLTS vs.\ TS).

\item \textbf{Ambiguity concentration.}  The calibration gap is largest in Q4
  (high annotation entropy), corresponding primarily to the
  Mel$\leftrightarrow$NV confusion region.  This is precisely the clinically
  critical zone where miscalibration is most dangerous.
\end{enumerate}

% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\subsection{Skin Disease Classification (ISIC 2019)}
\label{sec:isic2019}
% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

We now present the experiment that most directly mirrors \citet{stutz2023conformal},
who study conformal prediction under the same ambiguous-ground-truth challenge using
the skin disease reader study of \citet{liu2020deep}.
We use the publicly available \textbf{ISIC 2019} challenge dataset
\citep{codella2018skin,combalia2019bcn}, which covers 8 skin conditions
(MEL, NV, BCC, AK, BKL, DF, VL, SCC) across ${\approx}25\,000$
dermoscopic images --- a superset of the categories studied in the
\citeauthor{liu2020deep} system.

\paragraph{Annotator model.}
\citet{liu2020deep} deployed their system in a reader study with $K=9$ board-certified
dermatologists annotating 963 clinical cases, reporting an aggregate inter-reader
agreement of approximately $73\%$.  We replicate this setup with $K=9$ synthetic
dermatologist annotations per image drawn from a clinically calibrated $8\times 8$
confusion matrix (Table~\ref{tab:isic-confusion}).
Agreement rates and off-diagonal patterns are calibrated to match the per-condition
statistics reported in \citet{liu2020deep} (Table~2) and \citet{haenssle2018man}:
MEL and NV form the most confused pair (agreement $73\%$/$76\%$),
AK/BKL/SCC form a high-confusion cluster ($62$--$67\%$),
while DF and VL are easy to distinguish ($87\%$/$91\%$).
The weighted overall agreement is $73\%$, matching \citet{liu2020deep} exactly.

\paragraph{Setup.}
We fine-tune an \textbf{EfficientNet-B4} \citep{tan2019efficientnet} pretrained on
ImageNet-1k with class-weighted cross-entropy (NV constitutes ${\approx}67\%$ of
ISIC 2019 training images).
Following a stratified $70\%/15\%/15\%$ train/val/test split, the validation set
serves as the calibration set (${\approx}3\,750$ images) and the test set
(${\approx}3\,750$ images) for evaluation.

