\paragraph{Results.}
Table~\ref{tab:isic} and Figure~\ref{fig:isic} confirm the same pattern across
all three datasets, now at the largest scale and most directly replicating
the \citet{liu2020deep}/\citet{stutz2023conformal} setting:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Temperature direction reversal.}  TS finds $T=0.85<1$ (increasing
  confidence on hard labels), while SLTS finds $T=2.90>1$ (decreasing confidence).
  This confirms Proposition~\ref{prop:ts-bias} in the exact clinical setting of
  \citet{liu2020deep}: a model that \emph{looks} overconfident under hard evaluation
  is actually \emph{underconfident} under soft evaluation.

\item \textbf{Largest calibration gap in the benchmark.}  TS reduces $\ECE_h$
  to $2.3\%$ but leaves $\SCE$ at $11.9\%$ ($\Delta=+9.6$ pp) --- the
  largest calibration gap across all our experiments.  This reflects the
  higher inter-reader confusion ($K=9$, $73\%$ agreement) compared to
  DermaMNIST ($K=5$, $64.7\%$ agreement).

\item \textbf{Soft-label methods close the gap.}  SLTS reduces $\SCE$ to $5.0\%$
  ($58\%$ relative reduction vs.\ TS), and IR-Soft to $5.1\%$.
  The Brier score and NLL improvements are also the largest across all experiments.

\item \textbf{Per-class analysis reveals clinically meaningful structure.}
  TS is most miscalibrated for MEL ($22.1\%$) and NV ($18.4\%$) ---
  the MEL$\leftrightarrow$NV pair identified by \citet{liu2020deep} as
  the most consequential clinical confusion.  SLTS reduces these to $7.2\%$
  and $6.1\%$, while leaving low-confusion classes (DF, VL) nearly unchanged.

\item \textbf{Ambiguity concentration.}
  The calibration gap is concentrated in Q4 (high annotation entropy),
  corresponding to the MEL/NV and AK/BKL/SCC confusion clusters ---
  exactly the conditions where dermatologist disagreement is highest in
  \citet{liu2020deep}.
\end{enumerate}

% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

\paragraph{Relationship to conformal prediction.}
The analogy between our calibration gap and the coverage gap
\citep{stutz2023conformal} runs deep: in both cases, calibration against voted
labels creates a systematic bias toward overconfidence/undercoverage of the
majority class.  In conformal prediction, the fix is to sample annotations
(MCCP); our MCTS proposes the same fix for calibration.  The resulting
theoretical guarantees are also analogous: MCCP has finite-sample coverage
guarantees for the annotator distribution; SLTS/MCTS optimize proper scoring
rules with respect to $\pi(\cdot\mid x)$.

\paragraph{When does the gap matter?}
The calibration gap grows with annotation entropy (Proposition
\ref{prop:entropy-gap}).  In practice, the gap is large for:
(i)~medical image analysis, where clinicians regularly disagree on edge cases;
(ii)~natural language inference, where entailment is pragmatically ambiguous;
(iii)~any task where crowdsourced labels are used and annotators are rational
rather than noisy.  Our experiments show the gap is negligible for
unambiguous inputs (Q1 quartile) and large (3--5$\times$) for highly ambiguous
inputs.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
\emph{Annotation availability:} soft calibration requires multiple annotations
at calibration time.  Our annotation-count ablation (Section
\ref{sec:n-annotators}) shows $m\ge 5$ is sufficient, which is often feasible.
\emph{Annotation quality:} we assume annotators are rational.  If annotations
are noisy rather than reflecting genuine ambiguity, the soft labels $\pihat(x)$
are a noisy estimate of $\pi(x)$, potentially degrading calibration.
\emph{Evaluation metric:} $\SCE$ requires soft labels at test time; in
deployment where only hard labels are available, practitioners must rely on
alternative indicators of soft calibration quality.

% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

We introduced the problem of confidence calibration under ambiguous ground
truth, formalized soft calibration (Definition~\ref{def:soft-cal}), and
demonstrated a systematic calibration gap that arises when standard methods
are applied to voted labels.  The gap is the direct calibration analogue of
the coverage gap in conformal prediction and grows monotonically with
annotation entropy (Proposition~\ref{prop:entropy-gap}).

Our proposed methods—SLTS, MCTS, and soft-label non-parametric
calibration—are simple, post-hoc, and require no retraining.  On CIFAR-10H,
they reduce soft ECE by 45--63\% relative to TS while maintaining competitive
hard-label calibration.  We release code and pre-computed logits to facilitate
future research.

The results call for a reassessment of calibration methodology in tasks with
genuine label ambiguity: hard-label calibration metrics can be deeply
misleading, and soft calibration should become the standard evaluation protocol
wherever multiple annotations are available.

