\section{Proof of Proposition \ref{prop:ts-bias}}
\label{app:proof-ts}

Let $\mathcal{D}_\text{amb}=\{(x_i,y_i^*): y_i^*=c, x_i\in\mathcal{C}\}$ be
the subset of calibration examples from the ambiguous cluster $\mathcal{C}$,
where the voted label is always class $c$.  For these examples, the model
outputs logits $z_i$ with $\operatorname{softmax}(z_i)_c\approx p_\text{conf}$
for some $p_\text{conf}\in(0,1)$.

The TS loss on $\mathcal{D}_\text{amb}$ is:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L}_{\text{TS}}(T;\mathcal{D}_\text{amb})
  = -\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}_\text{amb}|}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{D}_\text{amb}}
    \log \operatorname{softmax}(z_i/T)_c.
\end{equation}
As $T\to 0^+$, $\operatorname{softmax}(z_i/T)_c\to 1$ (if $z_{ic}>z_{ik}$
for all $k\ne c$), so $\mathcal{L}_{\text{TS}}\to 0$.  Hence the gradient
$\partial\mathcal{L}_{\text{TS}}/\partial T > 0$ for all $T>0$ (decreasing
$T$ always decreases the loss toward zero).  The optimal $T^*$ balances the
contribution from ambiguous and unambiguous examples; but for any proportion of
ambiguous examples, $T^*\le T_\text{no-ambig}^*$ (the optimal temperature
without ambiguous examples).

In the extreme case where only ambiguous examples exist, $T^*\to 0$.  With a
mix, $T^*<1$ whenever $p_\text{conf}$ is below 1 and the model is already
reasonably accurate on hard labels.

In contrast, soft calibration minimises:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L}_{\text{SLTS}}(T;\mathcal{D}_\text{amb})
  = -\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}_\text{amb}|}\sum_i \sum_k \pihat_k(x_i)
    \log\operatorname{softmax}(z_i/T)_k,
\end{equation}
with $\pihat_c(x_i)=q<1$ and $\pihat_k(x_i)>0$ for some $k\ne c$.  The
minimum-loss $T^*$ satisfies $\operatorname{softmax}(z_i/T^*)_c\approx q<1$,
requiring $T^*>T_\text{TS}^*$, and in particular $T^*>1$ when
$\operatorname{softmax}(z_i)_c > q$.  $\square$

